Blocks World as a command-line application
==========================================

This example plans moves in the Blocks World.  A world is three stacks of
blocks.  One move takes the top block of a stack and puts it on another
stack.  The program finds a shortest plan from a start world to a goal
world.  Curry states what a legal move is.  Python parses the worlds,
drives the search, and draws the plan.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

Non-determinism as search.  The function move has one rule per source
stack:

    move (x:xs, ys, zs) = (xs, x:ys, zs) ? (xs, ys, x:zs)
    move (xs, y:ys, zs) = (y:xs, ys, zs) ? (xs, ys, y:zs)
    move (xs, ys, z:zs) = (z:xs, ys, zs) ? (xs, z:ys, zs)

The rules overlap, so every rule that matches applies.  Each rule offers a
choice of two target stacks with ?.  So move w means every legal move from
w at once.  The Curry side has no loop, no list of moves, and no search
state.

Failure prunes the search.  The call plan n start goal extends a trace one
move at a time.  A trace that repeats an earlier world fails.  A trace that
uses up its n moves before it reaches the goal fails too.  A failed branch
is not an error.  It produces no value.  The values of plan n are exactly
the traces of at most n moves that reach the goal.

Iterative deepening.  A bound of n moves may have no plan.  Python asks for
bound 0, then 1, then 2, and so on.  The first bound with a plan gives a
shortest plan.  Python takes one plan per bound and stops the search.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The driver go.py is a small application built with argparse:

    python go.py ABC// //ABC

A world is three stacks separated by slashes.  Write each stack from its
top block to its bottom block.  The driver checks the notation and builds
the Curry data itself.  Each block is the expression of a constructor,
curry.expr(Blocks.A).  A Python list whose first element is a Curry symbol
is an application, not a list, so a stack is a list of such expressions.
A Python tuple of three lists becomes the Curry tuple World.

One call,

    curry.eval(Blocks.plan, bound, start, goal, converter='topython')

is a generator of plans.  The driver takes the first one with next() and
discards the generator.  This stops the search.  A bound with no plan
yields zero values, so next() returns the default None, and the driver
tries the next bound.  The converter turns the trace into a Python list of
tuples of lists.  The blocks come back as Curry constructors.  str() of one
gives its name, and the driver uses that to draw the stacks.

The option --serve PORT starts a web form with http.server from the Python
standard library.  The form calls the same function solve() as the command
line does.  A Flask front end would call the same function.  The run
script does not start the server.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

To plan other problems, pass a start world and a goal world to go.py.  Use
the Python of the staged install.  For example, on the C++ backend:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ../../install/bin/python go.py ABCD// //ABCD

The blocks are A, B, C, D, and E.  The start and the goal must hold the
same blocks.  The option --max-moves N sets the bound at which the search
gives up.  The default is 12.

To use the web form, run

    ../../install/bin/python go.py --serve 8000

and open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ in a browser.  Press Ctrl-C to stop it.

The module has a goal main for sprite-exec.  It prints every plan of at
most five moves for the first problem of the run script.  There is one
plan, so the command prints one line:

    ../../install/bin/sprite-exec Blocks.curry
    [([A, B, C], [], []), ([B, C], [A], []), ([C], [B, ...], []), ([], ..., [C]), ([], ..., [B, C]), ([], [], [A, B, C])]

The worlds of a trace share their stacks, and the printer prints ... in
place of a list that it has printed before.  For example, [B, ...] stands
for [B, A], and a bare ... stands for a whole list printed before.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  The script plans
two problems.  The first carries A, B, C from the first stack to the third
stack in the same order.  The key lines are:

    start ABC//  goal //ABC
    bound 0: no plan
    bound 1: no plan
    bound 2: no plan
    bound 3: no plan
    bound 4: no plan
    shortest plan: 5 moves
      1. A to stack 2  BC/A/
      2. B to stack 2  C/BA/
      3. C to stack 3  /BA/C
      4. B to stack 3  /A/BC
      5. A to stack 3  //ABC

Each move names the block and the stack it moves to.  Then it shows the
world after the move in the input notation.  A picture of the trace
follows.  Each world is drawn as three stacks with the top block on top:

    step 0   step 1   step 2   step 3   step 4   step 5
    A . .    . . .    . . .    . . .    . . .    . . A
    B . .    B . .    . B .    . B .    . . B    . . B
    C . .    C A .    C A .    . A C    . A C    . . C
    1 2 3    1 2 3    1 2 3    1 2 3    1 2 3    1 2 3

The second problem reverses the stack onto the third stack.  It takes
3 moves:

    shortest plan: 3 moves
      1. A to stack 3  BC//A
      2. B to stack 3  C//BA
      3. C to stack 3  //CBA

The output is identical on both backends.  Each of the two problems has
exactly one shortest plan.  For a problem with several shortest plans, the
first one found may differ between the backends.


5. Limits
---------

The work grows with the bound.  Each world has up to six moves, and the
runtime keeps the open alternatives in memory.  Three blocks are cheap: a
warm run of ./run took 0.4 s.  Four blocks, ABCD// to //ABCD, need 7
moves.  That took 1.9 s.  Five blocks, ABCDE// to //ABCDE, ran out of
memory under a 2 GB cap after 16 s.  So keep to four blocks.

The first run also compiles the module, which took about half a second
more.
